<article class="env-lemma" id="07ND">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07ND">Lemma <span data-tag="07ND">10.140.9</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be an injective finite type ring map with $R$ and $S$ domains. Then $R \to S$ is smooth at $\mathfrak q = (0)$ if and only if the induced extension $L/K$ of fraction fields is separable. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume $R \to S$ is smooth at $(0)$. We may replace $S$ by $S_ g$ for some nonzero $g \in S$ and assume that $R \to S$ is smooth. Then $K \to S \otimes _ R K$ is smooth (Lemma <a href="/tag/00T4" data-tag="00T4">10.137.3</a>). Moreover, for any field extension $K'/K$ the ring map $K' \to S \otimes _ R K'$ is smooth as well. Hence $S \otimes _ R K'$ is a regular ring by Lemma <a href="/tag/00TT" data-tag="00TT">10.140.3</a>, in particular reduced. It follows that $S \otimes _ R K$ is a geometrically reduced over $K$. Hence $L$ is geometrically reduced over $K$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/04KN" data-tag="04KN">10.43.3</a>. Hence $L/K$ is separable by Lemma <a href="/tag/030W" data-tag="030W">10.44.2</a>. 
    <p>
      Conversely, assume that $L/K$ is separable. We may assume $R \to S$ is of finite presentation, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00FG" data-tag="00FG">10.30.1</a>. It suffices to prove that $K \to S \otimes _ R K$ is smooth at $(0)$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00TG" data-tag="00TG">10.137.17</a>. This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/00TV" data-tag="00TV">10.140.5</a>, the fact that a field is a regular ring, and the assumption that $L/K$ is separable. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>